===Etymology 2===
{{ja-kanjitab|あららぎ|yomi=k}}
First appears in the ''{{w|Engishiki}}'' compilation of laws created in the early 900s, spelled phonetically in kanji as {{lang|ja|阿良良伎}}.<ref name="NKD"/> Spelled as {{m|ja|蘭}}, ''araragi'' literally means “wild onion” and refers to the wild [[rocambole]], possibly {{vern|hardneck garlic}}, notable for its tall green stalk sticking straight up. Used as a [[euphemism]] for {{lang|ja|塔}} (''tafu'' at the time),<ref name="NKD"/><ref name="DJR"/> as that word's Buddhist connotations were viewed as [[taboo]] in contexts having to do with the religious center of ''Itsuki no Miya'' ({{l|ja|斎宮}}, also known as ''{{w|Saikū}}'').

